  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1140 PM CDT THU JUL 30 2026\n\nAfternoon showers and thunderstorms remain in the forecast for\ntoday, as the stalled frontal boundary slowly starts to lift\nnorth. This retrograding is ahead of a deep trough and low\npressure slated to move into the region via the Midwest over the\nweekend. As this trough begins to move into the region on\nSaturday, it will be met with an incredibly moist airmass, with\nPWAT values over 2 inches in many locations. Depending on how\nprogressive this trough is, periods of heavy rainfall should\nremain in the forecast through Sunday, with multiple rounds of\nheavy thunderstorms anticipated. Portions of the region are also\nhighlighted in a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) for severe weather\non Saturday, with damaging winds being the primary threat with\nany strong to severe activity.\n\nBy Monday, this trough should become closed up from the main flow\nacross southern Canada, meandering across the eastern part of the\ncountry into the middle of the weekend. This will keep higher\nrain chances in the forecast given the increased forcing. With\nthat being said, this trough should dissipate by Thursday under a\nbuilding ridge to our west. This will allow for afternoon highs\nto begin climbing once again, right back into the low and mid-90s\nheaded into next weekend.\n\n/44/",
